{#- One day of a learning cycle, with the reading itself inside it.

    The parasha's anatomy without its hero. A portion is a week and can carry a
    photograph; a day is a day, and somebody who has come for today's two mishnayot has
    come to read them, not to be sold the idea. So the page opens on the date and the
    reference and then gets out of the way.

    Nothing here is generated day to day. A calendar says which range, a cut takes it out
    of a text already on the shelf, and the page points at a reader built by a cron. -#}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
{% include '_public_head.html.j2' %}
<style>{{ asset('reader.css') }}{{ asset('public.css') }}</style>
{{ hebrew_face(true) }}
<script>{{ asset('theme.js') }}</script>
</head>
<body class="public">
<main class="sheet weekly parasha daily">
  <div class="masthead">
    <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="targum">
      <svg class="brand-mark" viewBox="20 12 56 72" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        <rect x="20" y="12" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="currentColor"></rect>
        <rect x="54" y="22" width="22" height="62" rx="5" fill="var(--mark-accent)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <span>targum</span>
    </a>
    <div class="corner">
      <a class="enter" href="/account/signin">Sign in</a>
      <button type="button" data-theme-toggle aria-label="Switch theme"></button>
    </div>
  </div>

  {#- The hero. The parasha's band: a seam down the middle, words on the ink half and a
      picture on the other, so no word is ever set over the writing in the photograph.

      The picture is a manuscript of the text the cycle reads — the Kaufmann Mishnah, the
      Aleppo Codex, the Leningrad Codex, the Psalms scroll from Qumran — and not a
      portrait of whoever is behind it. See `assets/manuscripts/README.md`: there is no
      likeness of Judah the Prince to use, the one photograph of anything connected to him
      is ShareAlike, and every "portrait" of a sage of the Mishnah is a nineteenth-century
      invention. A page of the book is a picture of the book. -#}
  <section class="band">
    <div class="said">
      <span class="eyebrow">{% if is_today %}Today{% else %}{{ day.hdate }}{% endif %}</span>
      <h1 class="pitch">{{ cycle.name }}, every word explained.</h1>
      <span class="named" lang="he" dir="rtl">{{ cycle.hebrew }}</span>
      <p class="lede">{{ cycle.blurb }}</p>
    </div>
    {#- Decoration: it says nothing the words have not said, so it is hidden from a
        screen reader rather than described to one. What it is and who wrote it is said
        in words further down, where a reader can read it. -#}
    <div class="pic" aria-hidden="true">
      <img src="{{ data_uri('manuscripts/' ~ cycle.picture) }}" alt="">
    </div>
  </section>

  {#- What is read today, said twice: the way the cycle writes it and the way a Hebrew
      reader would. The Hebrew date under it, because that is the date this is keyed to
      and a page that only says "1 September" is a page about a Gregorian day. -#}
  <p class="dateline">
    <b lang="he" dir="rtl"><bdi>{{ day.hebrew or day.title }}</bdi></b>
    <span class="said-in-english">{{ day.title }}</span>
    <span class="when">{{ day.hdate }}</span>
  </p>

  <div class="embed" id="embed">
    <button type="button" class="handle" id="embed-handle" aria-expanded="false">Full screen</button>
    {#- The first section where there is more than one, and the whole thing where there
        is not — see `build.opens_at`. A day of Tehillim is seven psalms and its
        `index.html` is a list of seven links; a day of Mishna Yomi is one chapter and has
        no `sec-0001.html` to point at. -#}
    <iframe
      src="/{{ cycle.slug }}/read/{{ day.slug }}/reader/{{ opens }}"
      title="{{ day.title }}"
      loading="lazy"></iframe>
  </div>

  {#- The days either side, so somebody who missed yesterday can still read it and
      somebody looking ahead can. Only the ones actually built: the window rolls, and a
      link to a day that has fallen out of it is a link to a 404. -#}
  {% if nearby %}
  <nav class="nearby" aria-label="Other days">
    {%- for other in nearby %}
    <a class="day{% if other.day == day.day %} here{% endif %}"
       {% if other.day == day.day %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}
       href="/{{ cycle.slug }}/{{ other.slug }}">
      <span class="date">{{ other.hdate }}</span>
      <bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">{{ other.hebrew or other.title }}</bdi>
    </a>
    {%- endfor %}
  </nav>
  {% endif %}

  {#- The other cycles. Not a nav bar: four links, each saying what it reads today, which
      is the thing somebody deciding between them wants to know. -#}
  {% if others %}
  <section class="how">
    <h2 class="label">Also today</h2>
    <ol class="cycles">
      {%- for one, said in others %}
      <li>
        <b><a href="/{{ one.slug }}">{{ one.name }}</a></b>
        <span>{{ one.rhythm }} — today, <bdi lang="he" dir="rtl">{{ said }}</bdi></span>
      </li>
      {%- endfor %}
    </ol>
  </section>
  {% endif %}

  <section class="honest">
    <h2 class="label">Made honestly</h2>
    <p class="made">
      The Hebrew is the published edition, cut to today's reading and not edited. The
      English beside it is {{ translation_said }}. The cycle and its calendar come from
      Hebcal. The picture is {{ cycle.credit }}.
    </p>
    {#- What Hebcal publishes and this shelf cannot read. A list, because it was a
        sentence with semicolons in it and the full stops inside each reason ran into
        them. Somebody looking for Daf Yomi is owed the reason, not a paragraph. -#}
    {% if absent %}
    <dl class="absent">
      {%- for name, why in absent %}
      <dt>{{ name }}</dt>
      <dd>{{ why }}</dd>
      {%- endfor %}
    </dl>
    {% endif %}
  </section>

  {#- The door. The parasha's, exactly: `.go` is only paper-on-ink inside `.door`, and
      outside it the block inverts and the link does not — which is a blue underline on
      near-black, and §9's "a beige button on a beige card is forbidden" said the other
      way round. -#}
  <section class="keep">
    <h2 class="label">Keep what you learn</h2>
    <p class="section-title">Every word you mark, remembered.</p>
    <p class="pitch-line">In tomorrow's reading, and in everything you read after it.</p>
    <p class="door">
      <a class="go" href="/account/signin">Sign in</a>
      <span class="free">An email address. No password.</span>
    </p>
  </section>

</main>

<script>{{ asset('parasha.js') }}</script>

{% include '_public_foot.html.j2' %}
</body>
</html>
